        heading: "Hazard Reviews and Layers of Protection",
        body: "The structured hazard review is where process safety engineering begins. A multidisciplinary team — process, mechanical, electrical, and operations engineers — systematically examines each part of the ammonia system for deviations: more flow, less flow, more pressure, loss of cooling, loss of power, wrong composition. For each deviation with a credible path to a release, the team documents the causes, the consequences, and the safeguards already in place, then decides whether the remaining risk is acceptable. Gaps become action items that the design must close: add an interlock, upsize a relief valve, relocate a valve, add detection.\n\nLayers of protection are then designed to be independent. A pressure relief valve, an automatic high-pressure shutdown, and an operator response are three separate layers — the engineer designs them so a single failure cannot defeat all three. Relief devices are sized for the worst credible scenario, such as a fire engulfing a storage vessel or a blocked outlet with full refrigeration running. Gas detection is positioned based on dispersion modeling of credible releases, not guesswork, and it is tied to alarms, ventilation changes, and automatic isolation. The engineer documents each layer's reliability so the hazard review team can credit it properly.",
