        body: "Control buildings get full floodproofing treatment. The engineer designs below-flood-level walls for hydrostatic and hydrodynamic loads, specifies waterproofing systems rated for the exposure, and details every penetration — conduits, cable trenches, pipes — with watertight seals. Doors below the flood line get deployable flood shields or are eliminated in favor of elevated access. Cable trenches, a classic flood vulnerability, are designed with sealed covers, drainage, and sump systems so they do not become underground rivers delivering water to every cabinet in the yard.\n\nThe yard civil design completes the picture. Grading sheds water away from equipment and toward controlled outlets; riprap or articulated concrete armoring protects foundations and the grounding grid from erosion during high-velocity flow; access roads are built to remain passable for repair crews after the storm. Drainage is sized for the design storm with the yard assumed saturated — the engineer checks that ponding drains within the utility's required recovery window. Fencing and gates are designed to survive debris impact and remain secure, because a flooded substation with a breached fence is both an outage and a safety hazard.",
