        heading: "Panels, Connections, and the Wind System",
        body: "Each tilt-wall panel is designed as a reinforced concrete element carrying gravity loads from the roof plus out-of-plane wind pressure on its face. The engineer sizes the panel thickness and reinforcement for the combined bending and axial load, checks lifting and bracing stresses — the panel sees its highest stresses while being tilted up, not in service — and details the lifting inserts and brace connections. Panel joints are designed for the in-plane shear the lateral system demands, with the engineer verifying that the vertical joints can transfer diaphragm forces between adjacent panels.\n\nThe roof diaphragm and its connections complete the lateral system. The engineer designs the steel deck or other diaphragm for the wind shear, provides continuous chords at the diaphragm edges, and details the panel-to-diaphragm connection — typically weld plates or bolted angles cast into the panels — to deliver the forces without brittle failure. Collectors drag concentrated forces into the shear panels. Every connection is designed for the load path it actually carries: a connection that looks robust but cannot develop the panel's capacity is a failure waiting for the first major storm. The engineer also designs for the building's openings — dock doors and windows interrupt the panel system, and the remaining panels and headers are checked for the redistributed loads.",
