        body: "Site layout begins with the levee district's maps: the restricted zones, the permanent servitudes, and the access corridors the district requires. The engineer fits the entire project — building, parking, utilities, stormwater facilities — into the unrestricted area, and any element that must cross a servitude is designed to the district's standards with its own permit application. Utility crossings under or near the levee get engineered details: casing pipes, sealed annular spaces, and shutoff valves positioned so a utility failure cannot become a levee failure.\n\nDrainage design must respect the flood-control system. The project cannot discharge concentrated flows against the levee slope, cannot pond water where it would saturate the levee foundation, and cannot alter the interior drainage the levee and its pump stations manage. The engineer designs detention and conveyance that release at controlled rates to approved outfalls, checks the design against the district's drainage criteria, and coordinates with the local drainage authority — the parish or municipality — whose requirements overlay the levee board's. In Louisiana's flat terrain, getting water to leave the site without harming the levee is a genuine engineering exercise.",
