        heading: "Drainage, Floodplain, and Erosion Control",
        body: "The engineer designs the site grading to balance the competing needs of flat crane work areas and positive drainage. Shallow swales and ditches carry stormwater to detention or to the outfall, sized for the region's intense design storms with the site's soils assumed saturated. Where the site falls in a mapped floodplain, the engineer confirms the flood zone and base flood elevation, elevates temporary electrical gear, offices, and fuel storage accordingly, and designs any fill or grading to satisfy the floodplain development permit — including compensatory storage where the parish requires it.\n\nErosion and sediment control is engineered for the disturbed, heavily trafficked site. The engineer designs perimeter controls, stabilized construction entrances, and sediment traps sized for the design storm, and sequences them with the yard construction so controls are in place before the ground is disturbed. Louisiana's stormwater permit for construction activity requires a pollution prevention plan, and the engineer's erosion control design forms its technical core. During the turnaround itself, the controls are inspected and maintained — a plan the engineer writes into the specifications, because controls that are not maintained are decorations.",
